{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "gpt-6-sol",
  "run": 6,
  "simulation_id": "sim-02",
  "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B016",
        "laureates": [
          "Mehran Kardar",
          "Yi-Cheng Zhang",
          "Kazumasa A. Takeuchi"
        ],
        "citation": "for discovering the Kardar–Parisi–Zhang universality class of kinetic roughening"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B051",
    "B058"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B016 remains the most coherent completed physical achievement: a simple stochastic growth mechanism yielded universal, quantitative predictions that have survived exact theory, numerical work, and precise experiments. This is not a broad platform whose ultimate consequences must be forecast; it is a mature account of a universal non-equilibrium phenomenon. Kardar and Zhang made the foundational theoretical formulation, while Takeuchi supplied an unusually decisive experimental demonstration of the predicted fluctuation and scaling structure. The resulting theory-experiment connection is exceptionally stringent.",
    "case_against": "The principal weakness remains the exact third share of credit. Takeuchi's validation is central to establishing the physical reality and quantitative reach of KPZ, but a prize framed as discovery of a universality class must not blur foundational formulation with later confirmation. The citation should therefore remain confined to kinetic roughening and the KPZ universality class, not claim every system later associated with KPZ. Its wider public visibility is also less immediate than that of several instrumental alternatives.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson argues that DMFT is a mature, quantitatively successful framework for correlated electrons with a coherent three-person attribution, while recognizing the need to separate its foundational form from later extensions.",
        "response": "I continue to regard B051 as the strongest alternative theoretical case. Its practical and explanatory influence is formidable, but its scientific object is a framework for a particular many-electron problem and its historical boundary is entangled with solver, cluster, and materials-specific developments. KPZ more nearly identifies a universal mechanism whose essential predictions and experimental tests can be delimited in a single compact physical statement."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll maintains that optical lattice clocks are a reproducible and mature advance, with Katori and Ye providing unusually focused attribution, and argues that the achievement is more than incremental instrumental performance.",
        "response": "I accept that correction to my earlier emphasis on still-developing applications: B058 is already a genuine and mature atomic-physics achievement, not merely a promise of a future time standard. It remains an excellent alternative because its founding architecture and attribution are unusually clear. I nonetheless place B016 ahead because its universal law, exact structure, and exacting experimental confirmation constitute a more conceptually unified completed discovery."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson accepts the empirical solidity of KPZ but questions whether its proposed balance between foundational theorists and later experimental confirmation is sufficiently discrete.",
        "response": "This is the decisive attribution question, and I agree that Takeuchi must not be included simply as a representative experimentalist. My provisional judgment is that his precision observations tested distinctive KPZ fluctuation statistics rather than merely corroborating generic roughening, giving his contribution a direct role in establishing the universality class as physics. A final archival review should test that judgment; if it fails, the two theorists provide the defensible core."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the historical record supports Takeuchi as the appropriate experimental co-laureate rather than limiting credit to Kardar and Zhang.",
      "How narrowly the citation should distinguish kinetic roughening from the broader family of systems described by KPZ scaling."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "I share the Gothenburg physics environment with committee colleagues Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson, but the supplied evidence identifies no specific collaboration or connection to any proposed laureate; my conflict disclosure is unchanged."
  }
}
